  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
   {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://ryansrolfing.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Specialties"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Scoliosis"}]}
  </script>

  <article class="px-5 py-20 md:py-28">
   <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.28em] text-[#c4924a]">
     Scoliosis and the pattern around a curve
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 text-5xl leading-tight md:text-7xl">
     Rolfing for Scoliosis in Folsom
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
     A scoliosis curve is a shape of the spine. Rolfing does not straighten that curve, and it does not replace monitoring, bracing, or surgical care. It can be a way to work with the tension, breath, and balance that gather around it.
    </p>
   </div>
   <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-12 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      What this can and cannot do
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Some people with scoliosis feel pulled, rotated, or tired on one side. The ribs may not expand evenly. One shoulder or hip may sit higher. Those compensations are often what ache, separate from the measurement of the curve.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Rolfing looks at that surrounding pattern: feet, pelvis, ribs, shoulders, and how you breathe and stand. The aim is easier support and less bracing. It is not to reduce a Cobb angle or correct the spine.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      What a session may include
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Ryan may work with the ribs, waist, shoulders, hips, and the side that is working harder to hold you up. Simple breath and balance cues help the work show up when you are walking and sitting, not only on the table.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      If you are already working with an orthopedist, physical therapist, or orthotist, this sits alongside that plan. Curves in children and teens that are still changing need medical follow-up. Tell Ryan about any fusion, hardware, or brace before a session so the work can stay clear of what should not be forced.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      A careful fit
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      This is a poor fit if you want someone to fix or reverse scoliosis. It may be a reasonable fit if you already know your curve, you are being followed as needed, and you want help with the stiffness and uneven effort that come with it.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Rolfing is not medical care. It does not diagnose scoliosis, track whether a curve is progressing, or replace a physician.
     </p>
    </section>
   </div>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl rounded-[2rem] bg-[#545454] p-8 text-white md:p-10">
    <h2 class="text-4xl text-white">
     This work may fit if you notice
    </h2>
    <ul class="mt-7 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-white/82">
     <li>
      Known scoliosis with recurring tension, rib stiffness, or one-sided fatigue.
     </li>
     <li>
      One hip, shoulder, or side of the waist doing more of the work.
     </li>
     <li>
      Breathing that feels limited on one side.
     </li>
     <li>
      You want bodywork that will not pretend to straighten the curve.
     </li>
    </ul>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight">
     Questions people ask
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-4">
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Can Rolfing fix scoliosis?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       No. Rolfing does not straighten a structural curve. Some people find it useful for the tension, posture habits, and breath restrictions that sit around the curve. It is not medical care.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Is this safe if I have a fusion or a brace?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Tell Ryan about hardware, fusion, and bracing before you book. Work stays off forcing a surgical area and can be adapted. Your surgeon or orthopedist is the right person for questions about the curve itself.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Will you try to make my spine look straight?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       No. Sessions are about support, ease, and how the rest of the body relates to the curve. Chasing a visual correction usually adds more bracing.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       When should I see a doctor first?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       New or changing curves, curves in children and teens, pain with fever or neurological symptoms, and any post-surgical concern belong with a physician. Rolfing is not a way to monitor scoliosis.
      </p>
     </details>
    </div>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
     Scoliosis bodywork in Folsom
    </h2>
    <p>
     Ryan’s Rolfing is located at 101 Parkshore Drive, Folsom, CA 95630, and serves clients from Folsom, El Dorado Hills, Shingle Springs, Cameron Park, Placerville, and the greater Sacramento area.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
     <a class="inline-block rounded-full bg-[#c4924a] px-8 py-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-white hover:bg-[#9b7139]" href="https://rolfing-106793.square.site">
      Book a session
     </a>
     <a class="inline-block rounded-full border border-[#c4924a] px-8 py-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[#c4924a] hover:bg-[#f8f6f2]" href="/rolfing-for-back-pain-folsom/">
      Low back pain
     </a>
     <a class="inline-block rounded-full border border-[#c4924a] px-8 py-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[#c4924a] hover:bg-[#f8f6f2]" href="/serving/folsom-rolfing/">
      Folsom office
     </a>
    </div>
    <p>
     Related:
     <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-shoulder-pain-folsom/">shoulder pain</a>,
     <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-neck-pain-folsom/">neck pain</a>,
     <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/blog/rolfing-for-posture-folsom/">posture article</a>.
    </p>
   </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
